    "title": "Amy Madigan Returns to the Oscars Red Carpet 40 Years After Her First Appearance",
    "text": "2 min readForty years ago, in 1986, Amy Madigan was a first-time Oscar nominee for Best Supporting Actress for her performance in Twice in a Lifetime. This evening, she’s back at the Academy Awards, with her second nod: also in the Best Supporting Actress category, for horror film Weapons.Tonight, she arrived wearing a Dior outfit, Messika earrings, and sunglasses, and accessorized with an Omega timepiece.Arturo Holmes//Getty ImagesMadigan is nominated tonight.Advertisement - Continue Reading BelowArturo Holmes//Getty ImagesMadigan and her husband Ed Harris on the red carpet.“It was much smaller. It wasn’t all of this,” Madigan recalled of her first time as a nominee. “There was no social media. There were no podcasts. There was no this and no that.” At the 1986 Academy Awards, she wore a black dress with a sheer top, accessorizing with a matching necklace and earrings set. Like tonight, back then, she was joined by her husband Ed Harris.Ron Galella, Ltd.//Getty ImagesAmy Madigan and Ed Harris during 58th Annual Academy Awards.Advertisement - Continue Reading BelowRon Galella//Getty ImagesMadigan was a Best Supporting Actress nominee for Twice in a Lifetime.“I’m just kind of stunned by the whole journey that I’ve been on,” Madigan said after her nomination. “This is just kind of another piece of it that I was not expecting, but I’m just very happy and gratified and it’s just a reflection on the film Weapons and how people are responding to it. So that — nothing is a better feeling than that.”She added, “It just means that you can have a long career. And I’ve had a long career and I’m continuing to do that… People always say, ‘If you have a dream, follow it,’ et cetera. But those are true things when actors and writers and directors get up there and say that because it’s a long, arduous trip, it takes a lot of work. Don’t be deterred. And it’s really true. When a good piece of material comes by, the actors, they want to grab it by the throat. It’s exciting. That’s why we do what we do. So it’s all been kind of a whirlwind, but very exciting.”Emily Burack (she/her) is the Deputy Digital Editor for Town & Country, where she covers entertainment, celebrities, the royals, and a wide range of other topics. Before joining T&C, she was the deputy managing editor at Hey Alma, a Jewish culture site. Follow her @emburack on Instagram, Twitter, and other social media platforms.",
